# Copy to .env and fill in your own values.
#
# EmailJS lets src/services/feedback.ts relay "Help and comments" reports
# to the developer inbox with no backend of the app's own:
#
#   1. Create a free account at https://www.emailjs.com and connect an email
#      service (the inbox reports should land in).
#   2. Create an email template using {{kind}}, {{title}} and {{message}} as
#      the variables, with a fixed subject of your own choosing.
#   3. In Account > Security, enable "Allow API calls from non-browser
#      applications" - without it EmailJS refuses every call from the app,
#      the same way Web3Forms did. This puts the account in strict mode,
#      which is why the Private Key below is also required: with no browser
#      to check the origin of, EmailJS asks for it on every call instead.
#   4. Copy the service id, template id and Public Key from the dashboard;
#      the Private Key is under Account > Security > API Keys.
#
# Unlike the other three, the Private Key is meant to stay private - EmailJS
# just gives no other way to prove a serverless app is really the app. It
# still ships inside this client because there is nowhere else for it to
# live, same as the rest of this section; someone who extracts it from the
# APK could send through your template until you rotate it, but not read
# anything back or reach your inbox otherwise.
EXPO_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_SERVICE_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY=
EXPO_PUBLIC_EMAILJS_PRIVATE_KEY=
